  gears the first two episodes kept things  really uneasy and almost intense and i'm  not sure i would want that for nine  episodes straight i mentioned it in my  first video but i wanted to point it out  again how having us see that helly's  audi is all for taking the job makes her  any character so useful to kind of  understand how things work we see that  even when he's presented with evidence  that things might not be so great in the  basement mark still chooses to work  there as a severed employee there's  nothing to indicate that audi heli would  be any different the people who would  elect to have this procedure are so ripe  for exploitation from lumen everything  about it works in the corporation's  favor but there's also a subset of  employees who they could dupe into  thinking it's what they really want and  that desire makes them complicit they  become active participants which is a  bit more interesting than a story that's  just about a corporation screwing over  its workers  that all kind of ties into what i was  saying earlier about how mark doesn't  seem completely surprised about some of  these crazy ways she tries to get a  message out do they really have a bad  soap procedure because people try to  write notes on their skin have they  really had to pull notes out of people's  bodies and how would a code detector  even work i'm not sure but at this point  it isn't taking me out of it because it  feels like we're moving towards  something and that has me willing to  just sit back and enjoy the ride i think  the tv debate is something to remember  the idea that this is about choice i'm  not sure if the implication is that a  lot of other companies are already doing  this they've already adopted severance  departments but based on there being  protests and talk of legislation i'm  guessing that's going to be happening  soon it makes me wonder if it's not so  much about what they're doing in macro  data refinement but more about covering  up the inherent problems they would run  into while pioneering a procedure like  this if it's something that makes them  money and they could capitalize on that  further by it being adapted across the  country they would want to keep any  mishap secret when it wants to be funny  it's funny and i gotta say that i think  dylan is my favorite character which is  crazy when you think about what a  stacked cast this is zac cherry is doing  an outstanding job here where almost  everything he says is completely  memorable the inner office relationships  manage to hit that spot between this is  absurd and oh yeah this resembles every  office job i've ever worked irv  definitely goes to the bosses behind his  colleagues backs all the time this is  pointed out by the mention of waffle  parties and sticky notes in the  announcements and it's so funny that  there are only four of them so everyone  knows where this is coming from i like  that we got to see more from the  employees who aren't severed the whole  mug throwing incident was fantastic and  then that came full circle when she had  to have her meeting with the board i am  wondering why she took mark's wife's  candle and i guess they were reading  rickon's book to screen it before giving  it back to him i'm definitely looking  forward to seeing some more interactions  between the other departments whether  there are five of them or 30 and i think  i'll just leave it there let me know in  the comments what you thought about the  episode and what you're hoping to see  next week who's your favorite character  and what do you think is going on in the  basement of lumen industries please like  this video if you enjoyed it please  subscribe to my channel if you haven't  already and thanks for watching i'll  talk to you soon

